# Every text file is checked out with LF endings, on Windows too, and
# git decides text against binary by content (so zips and images stay
# as they are).
#
# A Windows checkout with git's default core.autocrlf=true would
# rewrite text files with CRLF, and this tree reads its own files on
# Linux: shell scripts run in images (`#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` fails
# with `env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory`), Markdown is embedded
# into the server binary and parsed line by line (a CRLF handbook.md
# would make the server panic at start), and golden files
# (internal/web/apitypes/testdata/*.json) are compared byte for byte by tests that `make test-vm` runs on a copy of the checkout.
# One rule covers them all, and whatever comes next.
*              text=auto eol=lf
